<p>
  Paid for a taxi, a client lunch or a software license out of your own pocket? File an <strong>expense claim</strong>. It goes through your workspace's approval workflow, lands in the
  books once approved, and is marked paid when the money is back in your account.
</p>

<h2>Filing a claim</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    Go to <.link navigate="/expenses">Expenses → My claims</.link>
    and click <strong>+ Expense claim</strong>.
  </li>
  <li>
    Give it a title (<em>Client visit to Kigali</em>) and add one row per receipt: date, category,
    what it was, where, and the amount. The total updates as you type.
  </li>
  <li>
    Click <strong>Submit for approval</strong>, or <strong>Save draft</strong>
    to finish later. Each claim gets a number like <code>EXP-2026-0001</code>.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  A claim moves through these statuses: <em>Draft</em>
  → <em>Awaiting approval</em>
  → <em>Approved</em>
  → <em>Paid back</em>. If an approver rejects it, it comes back to you as <em>Rejected</em>
  with their comment: edit it and click <strong>Resubmit</strong>. You can cancel a claim any time
  before it's approved.
</p>

<h2>Approving claims</h2>
<p>
  Claims waiting for you appear under <.link navigate="/approvals">Approvals</.link>, oldest
  first. Open one, check the receipts, then <strong>Approve</strong>
  or <strong>Reject</strong>
  (rejecting needs a reason). The <em>Approval</em>
  panel on every claim shows each step: who decided, when, and what they said.
</p>
<.callout>
  Nobody approves their own claim. If you're the only possible approver for a step, that step is
  skipped and the claim moves on.
</.callout>

<h2>Approval workflows</h2>
<p>
  Admins decide who approves under <.link navigate="/approvals/workflows">Approval workflows</.link>. A workflow is a chain: <strong>initiator → approver 1 → approver 2 → … → final approver</strong>. Each approver can be:
</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Approver</th><th>Who decides</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Initiator's manager</td><td>
        The person the claimant reports to in People (Reports to).
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Specific person</td><td>One named team member, e.g. your CFO.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Anyone in a group</td><td>
        Any one member of an <.link navigate="/approvals/groups">approval group</.link>
        such as Finance.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Any workspace admin</td><td>Any owner or admin of the workspace.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>
  Steps run in order and each one must approve; one rejection ends the approval. Use
  <strong>Applies from</strong>
  to route bigger claims differently: for example one workflow for all claims (manager only) and
  another from 1,000 (manager, then Finance, then the CFO). The workflow with the highest threshold
  not above the claim's total is used. With no workflow at all, any workspace admin can approve.
</p>
<.callout kind={:warning}>
  Changing a workflow only affects claims submitted afterwards. Claims already in approval keep
  the steps they started with.
</.callout>

<h2>Categories and the books</h2>
<p>
  Admins manage <.link navigate="/expenses/categories">Categories</.link>
  (Travel, Meals, Office…) and pick the expense account each one is booked to. When a claim is
  approved, Zippiker records the expense in those accounts and what you owe the employee in <em>Employee reimbursements</em>. When an admin clicks
  <strong>Pay back</strong>
  (choosing bank, cash or mobile money), that balance is cleared. Both entries show in the <.link navigate="/accounting/journal">Journal</.link>.
</p>
